--
N5008 [dcl.init.general]/9.1: to value-initialize an object of class type,
if the constructor selected to default-initialize it is not user-provided
(implicitly declared, or explicitly defaulted on its first declaration,
[dcl.fct.def.default]/5), the object is first zero-initialized and then
default-initialized.  Value-initialization is what `T()' / `T{}'
([expr.type.conv]/2, [dcl.init.general]/16.4), an empty braced-init-list
initializer ([dcl.init.list]/3.5) and `new T()' / `new T{}' ([expr.new])
perform.  The front end only ran the default constructor for a non-POD
class, so the members of `S()' were indeterminate; POD classes were already
zeroed.  libc++ 23's basic_string resets its representation with `__rep_ =
__rep()', a union whose variant member has a defaulted default constructor
(see cpp11_union_value_initialization_defaulted_member).  Fixed in round
163b.
